  <h2 id="s-1">The four POUR principles, in plain words</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Perceivable</strong> — everyone can perceive the content, including
    people using screen readers or enlarged text.</li>
    <li><strong>Operable</strong> — everyone can operate the interface, including
    people who use only a keyboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Understandable</strong> — the content and the interface make sense
    on first read.</li>
    <li><strong>Robust</strong> — the content works across browsers and assistive
    technologies, now and in the future.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="s-2">What has been done in this build</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every form control has a <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code> bound to its id.</li>
    <li>Every error message is text and is announced to assistive tech via
    <code>aria-invalid</code> and <code>aria-describedby</code>.</li>
    <li>Every task can be completed with the keyboard alone.</li>
    <li>Text contrast targets 4.5:1 or better.</li>
    <li>Status is never conveyed by colour alone — every status pill carries a
    text label.</li>
    <li>A skip link appears on focus so screen-reader and keyboard users can jump
    straight to the main content.</li>
    <li>Focus rings are visible on every interactive element.</li>
    <li>Every meaningful image has alternative text; decorative images have empty
    alt attributes.</li>
    <li>Mobile-first layout — works on small screens first, then scales up.</li>
    <li>Motion is reduced when your device requests it
    (<code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>).</li>
    <li>Touch targets are at least 44&times;44 pixels.</li>
    <li>Body text is at least 16 pixels.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="s-3">What is still incomplete</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We have not yet had an external WCAG 2.2 AA audit. Our statement of
    compliance is based on self-testing only.</li>
    <li>Some older pages use slightly denser prose than is ideal for readers with
    cognitive disabilities — we are rewriting the longest sections.</li>
    <li>We have not yet tested with every popular screen reader. NVDA and VoiceOver
    are the primary targets.</li>
    <li>Print stylesheet is provided, but we have not tested it on all printers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="s-4">Keyboard-only instructions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tab</strong> — move forward through links, buttons, and form fields.</li>
    <li><strong>Shift + Tab</strong> — move backward.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter</strong> — activate a link or a button.</li>
    <li><strong>Space</strong> — toggle a checkbox, or press a button.</li>
    <li><strong>Skip link</strong> — press Tab once when a page loads. A "Skip to
    main content" link appears at the top. Press Enter to jump past the navigation
    into the page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="s-5">How to report a barrier</h2>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:accessibility@cspc.edu.ph">accessibility@cspc.edu.ph</a>
  or tell any staff member at the College of Computer Studies or the IT office.
  You do not need to fill in any form. We will respond within five working days
  and, where possible, fix the barrier within one month.</p>

